// Runtime/RuntimeAbort.maxon — ⭐ **THE ONE HOME OF THE PROCESS EXIT CODES THE EMITTED RUNTIME ABORTS
// WITH**, and the one shape those aborts are emitted in.
//
// ⚠ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE SPACE HAD ALREADY COLLIDED.** `GtRuntime.ProcStoreOverflowExitCode` and
// `ArrayRuntime.ElementSizeZeroExitCode` were BOTH 70 — two unrelated aborts wearing one number, each
// picked against a different file-local list of "codes I can see from here". Nothing was broken by it
// (a program cannot reach both), but the next number added was a coin flip, and a runtime abort that
// reports another abort's code is a debugging session that starts by reading the wrong function.
//
// ⭐ **THE FIX IS THE SHAPE THE 4-DIGIT ERROR CODES ALREADY PROVE: ONE DECLARATION SITE PLUS A CHECK
// THAT FAILS THE BUILD ON A DUPLICATE** — except that here the check needs no tooling, because the
// LANGUAGE already is one. An enum's raw values must be distinct, so a second claimant on a number is
// **E3031 `duplicate raw value`** from the bootstrap that compiles this file: the collision cannot be
// committed, let alone shipped. That is why these are enum CASES and not seven `let`s.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS NOT THE 4-DIGIT ERROR REGISTRY AND MUST NOT BE FOLDED INTO IT.** `docs/error-codes.txt`
// numbers DIAGNOSTICS the compiler prints at compile time; these are PROCESS EXIT CODES a compiled
// program dies with at run time. Different space, different consumers (a shell / a spec's `exitcode`
// block), different lifetimes.
//
// ⭐ **HOW TO PICK THE NEXT ONE: take the next free number ABOVE 70.** The band below is not ours —
// 0-69 are ordinary program exit codes (a Maxon `main` returns one), and the subprocess specs' children
// already use 1/2/3/4/5/25/45/123 — so an abort down there is indistinguishable from a program that
// simply returned. (`runProcess`'s two failures used to break that rule with aborts 1 and 70; since
// P1.5 #93 they THROW `RunProcessError` and the caller catches them, so no abort sits below 71 any more.)
// 101 is the one code the compiler already spends outside this band and it is declared here so the
// enum's own duplicate check covers it.
//
// ⚠ **134 IS NOT HERE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS DELIBERATE.** It was `userPanic`, the code a user `panic("…")`
// exited with while the message was going unprinted; the panic statement now routes to the SAME
// `mrt_panic` runtime the range-check guard uses (`StdOp.osPanic`), which exits `PanicExitCode` — so
// nothing emits 134 any longer. A case nothing emits is a DEAD CLAIM, the same rot as a code that
// reports another abort's number, so it was deleted rather than kept as a number this space defends
// against a claimant that no longer exists.
export enum RuntimeAbort
	// `__arr_grow`: `capacity·element_size` overflowed i64. Without the guard the wrapped byte count
	// under-allocates and the next element write runs off the buffer — a silent heap overflow.
	arrayCapacityOverflow = 71
	// `__arr_append`: `dest` and `src` disagree on `element_size@24`, so the single-stride blit would
	// over-read the packed source and splice garbage in.
	arrayAppendElementSizeMismatch = 72
	// `__arr_resize`: a NEGATIVE length. The shrink's zero range starts at `buffer + n·es`, which for a
	// negative `n` is an address BELOW the buffer — the one out-of-buffer write a detach cannot fix.
	// Pinned by `byte-string-literal.negative-resize-aborts`.
	arrayNegativeResize = 73
	// `__arr_create`: a 0 element size, which is a COMPILER bug rather than a program's (a concrete
	// literal is never 0 and a descriptor read never yields 0) — it would make `__arr_grow` allocate a
	// zero-byte buffer and corrupt. ⚠ It was 70 until this registry landed, colliding with the
	// process-store-overflow abort that then held 70 (since P1.5 #93 that path throws
	// `RunProcessError.storeOverflow` instead); this is the one that moved because it is unreachable from
	// any source program.
	arrayElementSizeZero = 74
	// `__mm_leak_check` (the ONE OS-exit gate): a green thread was spawned but neither AWAITED nor DROPPED —
	// `__gt_live_count` was non-zero at exit (or, negative, an over-reclaim). A GT struct + its seed stack are
	// `__slab_alloc`/`osAllocPages` allocations INVISIBLE to `__mm_alloc_count`, so this leak is its own class
	// and its own code — DISTINCT from `leakDetected` (101, the heap gate) so a debugger reading the exit code
	// goes to the green-thread counter, not the heap one (this file's founding rule: an abort must not report
	// another abort's number). Pinned by `async-promise-drop`'s never-ran/completed/parked cases.
	greenThreadLeak = 75
	// `__set_insert` / `__map_upsert`: the linear probe walked all `capacity` slots without finding an Empty
	// one AND saw no Deleted tombstone to reuse — i.e. count == capacity. UNREACHABLE from any source program:
	// insert grows (to <75% load, so >=1/4 of slots are non-Occupied) BEFORE probing, and a fresh table has no
	// tombstones, so an Empty slot is always found first. It fires only on a broken load-factor/grow invariant
	// — a COMPILER bug — where the alternative (a silent `retVoid`) would drop the entry and leave `count`
	// lying. Same "unreachable from source, abort not silence" class as `arrayElementSizeZero`.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONE CODE FOR BOTH TABLES, because there is one INSERT** — `HashTableRuntime.buildHtInsert` builds
	// the set's and the map's from the same graph, so a second number here would be two claimants on one
	// `osExit` site, which is this registry's founding complaint wearing the opposite hat.
	hashTableInsertNoFreeSlot = 76
	// `__strix_slice`: the byte range `s.slice(start, endIndex: end)` resolved to is not a range of this
	// string — a negative start, an INVERTED range, or an end past `length@8`. Reachable from source
	// (`s.slice(idx, endIndex: s.startIndex())`, or an index taken from a LONGER string), and without the
	// guard the negative length reaches `__mm_alloc` as an under-sized request followed by an
	// out-of-buffer read. The reference dies on the same input: `String.sliceBytes` forwards to a throwing
	// `managed.slice` under an `otherwise panic(…)`, so this abort matches the oracle's CLASS of answer
	// (the process ends) though not its message — shv2's `mrt_panic` prints on the two x64 lanes (rung
	// A1j added x64-linux), on both arm64 lanes (A3n) and on wasm (W3) — so the message now reaches
	// stderr on EVERY target, and the difference from the oracle is the message TEXT alone.
	stringSliceInvalidRange = 77
	// `__mf_stat_field` / `__mf_stat_free`: a NULL stat buffer. Both references treat it as a stdlib-only
	// INVARIANT violation rather than a catchable error (`__ManagedFile.statField`/`statFree` are registered
	// non-throwing in each), and `__mm_free` is not null-safe — without the guard the free writes to the
	// header eight bytes below address 0.
	managedFileStatNullBuffer = 78
	// `__mf_stat_field`: an index outside the six fields the 48-byte stat buffer holds — including a
	// NEGATIVE one, which the signed `StdCmpPred` makes a separate compare. Without the guard the read
	// returns whatever sits past a 48-byte allocation: a silent wrong answer, not a crash.
	managedFileStatIndexOutOfRange = 79
	// `__str_byte_at_or_panic`: the index is not a byte position of this string — negative, or at or past
	// `length@8`. The method's whole contract is that the CALLER has already bounds-checked
	// (`stdlib/String.maxon:281-283` is `try byteAt(index) otherwise panic(…)`), so an out-of-range index is
	// a stdlib bug and the process ends. ⭐ It gets its own number rather than riding the user-panic route for
	// the reason `stringSliceInvalidRange` above got one: the reference's abort is spelled `panic(…)` in stdlib
	// SOURCE, but shv2 answers it from a SYNTHESIZED runtime function, which has no source line and no message
	// to bake — so it cannot reach `osPanic` and reports its own number instead. Same CLASS of answer as the
	// oracle (the process ends), not the same message.
	stringByteAtOutOfRange = 80
	// `__mm_leak_check`: the live allocation count was not zero at exit — a LEAK, or (a negative count)
	// an over-release. ⚠ **`Testing/SpecWorkerPool.LeakCheckExitCode` SPELLS 101 TOO, AND FOLDING IT INTO
	// THIS CASE WOULD BE WRONG.** That constant reads the exit code of a WORKER, and a worker is the shv2
	// binary — compiled by the C# BOOTSTRAP, so the 101 it reads is emitted by `maxon-sharp`'s leak gate,
	// not by the runtime this enum describes. Pointing the harness at this case would make it look
	// single-sourced while its actual producer sat in another compiler. 101 is a PROJECT-WIDE CONVENTION
	// with three independent producers; this enum owns shv2's, and its job here is to stop a second shv2
	// abort taking the number.
	leakDetected = 101
end 'RuntimeAbort'

// ⭐ **THE PANIC LINE ITSELF — `panic at <file>:<line>: <text>\n` — WRITTEN ONCE.**
//
// THREE producers assemble a message for `mrt_panic`, and they must not each spell the format: the P1.9
// range-check guard (`InsertRangeChecks.rangeCheckMessage`), a user `panic("…")`
// (`Parser.parsePanicStatement`), and an INTERPOLATED `panic("…{x}…")`, which cannot call this function
// at all because its text is not known until the program runs — it takes the two halves below instead
// (`Parser.renderedPanicMessagePointer`). They differ ONLY in the TEXT after the colon — "Range check
// failed: …" against whatever the program wrote — so the location prefix, the separator and the trailing
// newline are this file's, and no caller restates them. `mrt_panic` prints the result verbatim, then
// `Stack trace:` and its frames, then exits `PanicExitCode`.
export function panicMessageLine(filePath FilePath, line LineNumber, text String) returns ByteArray
	var baked = panicMessageLinePrefix(filePath, line: line)
	baked.append(text.toByteArray())
	baked.append(PanicMessageLineTerminator)
	return baked
end 'panicMessageLine'

// ⭐ **THE LINE'S TWO CONSTANT HALVES, NAMED, because a THIRD producer needs them SEPARATELY.** An
// interpolated `panic("value is {x}")` cannot bake its line — `{x}` is unknown until the program runs —
// so the parser assembles it as an ordinary interpolation with these two as extra literal fragments
// (`Parser.renderedPanicMessagePointer`). Splitting the format here rather than letting that path spell
// `"panic at …: "` a second time is the whole reason this function exists: the location prefix, the
// separator and the terminator stay written down ONCE, whichever of the three producers assembles them.
export function panicMessageLinePrefix(filePath FilePath, line LineNumber) returns ByteArray
	return "panic at {baseName(filePath.toString())}:{line}: ".toByteArray()
end 'panicMessageLinePrefix'

// ⚠ **THE NEWLINE IS PART OF THE MESSAGE, NOT OF THE RUNTIME** — `mrt_panic` writes the cstr it is handed
// and starts the backtrace header immediately after, so a producer that omitted it would run the message
// into `Stack trace:` on one line.
export let PanicMessageLineTerminator = b"\n"

// The base file name (last path segment) of a source path, for the runtime panic message — the shv2
// equivalent of the bootstrap's `Path.GetFileName`. Strips both separators so a Windows temp path and a
// POSIX one both reduce to `<name>.test`.
function baseName(path String) returns String
	return lastSegment(lastSegment(path, separator: "/"), separator: "\\")
end 'baseName'

function lastSegment(s String, separator String) returns String
	let parts = s.split(separator)
	if parts.isEmpty() 'whole'
		return s
	end 'whole'
	return try parts.get(parts.count() - 1) otherwise s
end 'lastSegment'

// ⭐ **ABORT THE PROCESS FROM A BUILDER-BUILT RUNTIME FUNCTION — THE ONE HOME OF THE ABORT SHAPE.**
// `osExit` DIVERGES, so the block it sits in never falls out; but a block must still carry a
// terminator, so a DEAD one follows. That fact was open-coded four times (`ArrayRuntime.emitVoidAbort`
// plus `GtRuntime`'s `__gt_proc_add` and `__gt_process_run`, and `ArrayRuntime.__arr_create`), and it
// is written here once.
//
// ⭐ **THE TERMINATOR KIND IS DERIVED FROM THE FUNCTION, NOT PASSED IN.** A void function's dead
// terminator is `retVoid` and a value-returning one's is `ret <0>`; that is exactly what
// `func.returnType` already says, so it is read rather than restated. A caller therefore cannot pick
// the wrong one — which is the failure the previous split into a void helper plus two open-coded
// value-returning copies left available. (A THROWING runtime function returns `value(i64)` through the
// dual-register ABI; its `ret` is dead too, so the same answer is right there.)
export function emitRuntimeAbort(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, abort RuntimeAbort, ids ValueMinter)
	// Bound to a local FIRST: the bootstrap's union-case argument type-checker cannot lower an
	// `EnumMember.rawValue` written directly as a case argument (E9001) — the same reason
	// `StdToArm64Conversion.vregOf` binds `reg.rawValue` before using it.
	let code = abort.rawValue as ParsedInt
	let codeId = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(codeId, value: code, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osExit(codeId))

	match func.returnType 'deadTerminator'
		void then module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.retVoid)
		value then emitDeadValueReturn(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	end 'deadTerminator'
end 'emitRuntimeAbort'

// The dead `ret 0` a value-returning function's abort block carries. The value is unread by
// construction — `osExit` above it never returns — so a plain 0 is the honest filler. It is a function
// rather than two lines inside the arm above because a `match` arm is ONE statement.
function emitDeadValueReturn(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let deadId = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(deadId, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.ret(deadId))
end 'emitDeadValueReturn'
